1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,010
People say that exercise is good for you.

2
00:00:03,675 --> 00:00:05,685
But have you ever stopped to ask why?

3
00:00:06,155 --> 00:00:08,605
Why does your body
actually need you to move?

4
00:00:09,469 --> 00:00:11,539
We do not have to walk to get water.

5
00:00:12,129 --> 00:00:14,219
We do not have to go looking for food.

6
00:00:14,829 --> 00:00:16,959
We do not have to walk miles to work.

7
00:00:17,569 --> 00:00:20,579
We do not even have to stand
up to change the channel.

8
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:25,135
And yet doctors keep saying
the same thing. Move more.

9
00:00:25,903 --> 00:00:28,293
So what exactly goes wrong when we don't?

10
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:34,823
In 1966, five healthy young
men agreed to an experiment.

11
00:00:35,653 --> 00:00:37,263
They were twenty years old.

12
00:00:37,938 --> 00:00:42,748
For three weeks, they stayed in bed.
Not ill. Not injured. Just horizontal.

13
00:00:43,689 --> 00:00:45,569
Then the researchers measured how much

14
00:00:45,649 --> 00:00:48,259
oxygen their bodies
could use at full effort.

15
00:00:49,097 --> 00:00:51,057
It had fallen by twenty-seven per cent.

16
00:00:51,137 --> 00:00:52,927
In three weeks.

17
00:00:54,297 --> 00:00:56,127
The heart was the main reason.

18
00:00:56,917 --> 00:01:00,907
Every beat was moving about a quarter less
blood than it had three weeks earlier.

19
00:01:01,669 --> 00:01:05,939
And then the study did something
almost no study does. It waited.

20
00:01:06,994 --> 00:01:11,744
The same five men came back thirty
years later. They were fifty now.

21
00:01:12,294 --> 00:01:16,287
Three decades of ordinary ageing
had cost them twelve per cent.

22
00:01:16,737 --> 00:01:21,567
Three weeks in bed at twenty had done more
damage than thirty years of getting older.

23
00:01:24,837 --> 00:01:27,027
That number is really the whole video.

24
00:01:27,137 --> 00:01:30,127
Because it tells you what
kind of thing a body is.

25
00:01:30,908 --> 00:01:35,758
A body is not a lump of matter that holds
its shape until something breaks it.

26
00:01:36,401 --> 00:01:39,091
It is a system that keeps
asking one question.

27
00:01:39,941 --> 00:01:41,851
How much am I being asked to do?

28
00:01:42,601 --> 00:01:44,531
Muscle is the obvious case.

29
00:01:45,141 --> 00:01:50,111
Stop loading it, and it does not simply
stay as it was. It gets taken apart.

30
00:01:51,063 --> 00:01:55,513
In bed rest studies, leg muscle can
go at roughly half a per cent a day.

31
00:01:56,237 --> 00:01:58,587
Bone does the same thing, more slowly.

32
00:01:59,557 --> 00:02:01,837
Astronauts in orbit lose bone density at

33
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:04,167
something like one to
two per cent a month.

34
00:02:04,953 --> 00:02:07,563
And here is the detail
that gives the game away.

35
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:09,883
They do not lose it evenly.

36
00:02:10,769 --> 00:02:14,919
The arms, which never carried their own
weight anyway, lose much less of it.

37
00:02:15,829 --> 00:02:18,819
The hips and the spine, which
used to, lose much more.

38
00:02:19,507 --> 00:02:22,537
The body is not decaying. It is measuring.

39
00:02:22,947 --> 00:02:25,777
And it is measuring where
the load actually goes.

40
00:02:28,347 --> 00:02:32,457
And you do not need a hospital bed
or a spacecraft to watch this happen.

41
00:02:33,157 --> 00:02:35,637
Ten healthy young men were
asked to do one thing.

42
00:02:35,717 --> 00:02:37,147
Walk less.

43
00:02:38,013 --> 00:02:41,393
They went from about ten thousand steps
a day to roughly thirteen hundred.

44
00:02:41,473 --> 00:02:43,483
For two weeks.

45
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:46,220
By the end of it, their muscles had become

46
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,250
measurably worse at pulling
sugar out of the blood.

47
00:02:50,047 --> 00:02:53,257
About a sixth worse, on the
measure the researchers used.

48
00:02:53,827 --> 00:02:57,977
Their fitness had dropped as well, and
so had the lean mass in their legs.

49
00:02:58,827 --> 00:03:01,257
Nobody was ill. Nobody was in bed.

50
00:03:01,907 --> 00:03:03,857
They had simply stopped walking to things.

51
00:03:06,571 --> 00:03:08,841
So why would a body be built like that?

52
00:03:09,675 --> 00:03:13,405
Because for almost all of our
history, there was no way to opt out.

53
00:03:14,229 --> 00:03:16,739
If they wanted food,
they had to get to it.

54
00:03:17,409 --> 00:03:19,839
If they wanted to be
somewhere else, they walked.

55
00:03:20,706 --> 00:03:23,076
They carried things. They
worked with their hands.

56
00:03:23,726 --> 00:03:25,216
They walked, ran and climbed.

57
00:03:26,116 --> 00:03:29,093
And this is the part worth holding on to.

58
00:03:29,276 --> 00:03:30,906
Movement was not exercise.

59
00:03:31,296 --> 00:03:33,066
It was just what a day looked like.

60
00:03:35,286 --> 00:03:36,976
And we can put a number on that.

61
00:03:37,726 --> 00:03:40,046
The Hadza are a hunting and
gathering people in Tanzania.

62
00:03:40,126 --> 00:03:43,876
Forty-six of them wore
heart rate monitors.

63
00:03:44,922 --> 00:03:47,122
They spent about two
and a quarter hours of

64
00:03:47,202 --> 00:03:49,492
every day at moderate to vigorous effort.

65
00:03:50,351 --> 00:03:52,331
The health guidelines
most countries publish

66
00:03:52,411 --> 00:03:54,351
ask for a hundred and
fifty minutes of that.

67
00:03:54,431 --> 00:03:56,361
Per week.

68
00:03:57,169 --> 00:03:59,839
The Hadza pass the weekly
target in two days.

69
00:04:02,579 --> 00:04:06,019
Meanwhile, a study that counted
steps on seven hundred thousand

70
00:04:06,099 --> 00:04:10,169
phones put the global average
at about five thousand a day.

71
00:04:10,825 --> 00:04:13,355
Now think about an ordinary working day.

72
00:04:14,163 --> 00:04:18,213
You get up. You sit in the car. You
sit at work. You sit at a screen.

73
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:20,531
You sit down for lunch.

74
00:04:21,161 --> 00:04:22,911
You sit in the car going home.

75
00:04:23,581 --> 00:04:25,951
You sit in front of another
screen in the evening.

76
00:04:26,735 --> 00:04:30,545
Not one moment of it told your body that
it needed to be capable of anything.

77
00:04:33,645 --> 00:04:37,395
Now, this is the point where this
kind of story usually goes wrong.

78
00:04:38,093 --> 00:04:40,423
Because it is very easy to turn it into:

79
00:04:40,693 --> 00:04:43,583
everything old was good,
everything new is bad.

80
00:04:44,375 --> 00:04:48,085
It was not. Hunter-gatherers
were not healthier at everything.

81
00:04:49,175 --> 00:04:53,705
Infections, injuries and childbirth were
dangerous in ways they are not for us.

82
00:04:54,395 --> 00:04:58,125
And there is one result that
complicates the story even further.

83
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:01,471
When researchers measured
how much energy the Hadza

84
00:05:01,551 --> 00:05:05,301
actually burn in a day, it came
out about the same as ours.

85
00:05:06,171 --> 00:05:08,661
Same body size, same daily total.

86
00:05:09,151 --> 00:05:12,101
All that walking did not
show up as a bigger number.

87
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:15,310
So this is not a story about burning more.

88
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:19,030
The body seems to spend a
similar budget either way.

89
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:22,670
It is a story about what it
is being asked to do with it.

90
00:05:25,650 --> 00:05:27,660
Which brings up the strangest part.

91
00:05:28,550 --> 00:05:31,660
If movement matters that much,
why does it not feel like it?

92
00:05:32,383 --> 00:05:35,853
You know when you are hungry.
You know when you are thirsty.

93
00:05:36,123 --> 00:05:37,593
You know when you have not slept.

94
00:05:38,497 --> 00:05:40,777
All of those come with
an alarm, because in

95
00:05:40,857 --> 00:05:43,227
each case the trouble arrives within days.

96
00:05:44,139 --> 00:05:46,729
There is no equivalent
alarm for not moving.

97
00:05:47,219 --> 00:05:50,309
What your body does instead
is quietly lower the target.

98
00:05:52,909 --> 00:05:55,859
Capacity you are not using
is expensive to keep.

99
00:05:56,069 --> 00:05:57,759
So it is let go.

100
00:05:58,575 --> 00:06:00,945
Which means everything still feels fine.

101
00:06:01,715 --> 00:06:04,745
Because fine is now measured
against a day of sitting down.

102
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,970
You only find out when you ask for
something you have not asked for in years.

103
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,830
Two flights of stairs. A run for a bus.

104
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:19,170
And then there is the second half
of it, which is almost unfair.

105
00:06:20,088 --> 00:06:21,978
We did not only evolve to move.

106
00:06:22,168 --> 00:06:25,518
We evolved to avoid moving
when we did not have to.

107
00:06:26,245 --> 00:06:27,495
Energy was scarce.

108
00:06:28,125 --> 00:06:32,715
Anything you spent walking for no reason
was energy not spent on staying alive.

109
00:06:33,575 --> 00:06:37,525
So the voice that says not today is not
laziness that arrived with the sofa.

110
00:06:38,595 --> 00:06:40,885
It is an old instinct
that used to be right.

111
00:06:44,015 --> 00:06:46,545
Now for a question that
is pure speculation.

112
00:06:47,035 --> 00:06:49,585
And it is worth saying so out loud.

113
00:06:50,427 --> 00:06:53,957
What if sitting had always
been normal? Not for decades.

114
00:06:54,347 --> 00:06:55,717
Not for a few generations.

115
00:06:56,087 --> 00:06:58,717
But for hundreds of thousands of years.

116
00:06:59,573 --> 00:07:03,223
Food always within reach.
Transport everywhere.

117
00:07:03,993 --> 00:07:06,623
Survival depending on how
well you handled information.

118
00:07:07,737 --> 00:07:11,647
Given that much time, evolution
would have something to work with.

119
00:07:12,334 --> 00:07:13,884
Maybe the muscle would go.

120
00:07:14,694 --> 00:07:16,744
Maybe the skeleton would be built lighter.

121
00:07:17,614 --> 00:07:20,564
Maybe the whole way we store
and spend energy would shift.

122
00:07:21,458 --> 00:07:23,768
We do not know what that
human would look like.

123
00:07:24,418 --> 00:07:26,588
Anyone who tells you they do is guessing.

124
00:07:29,318 --> 00:07:30,828
But the guess is not the point.

125
00:07:31,558 --> 00:07:33,418
The point is the timescale.

126
00:07:33,878 --> 00:07:36,628
Our surroundings changed
in about three generations.

127
00:07:37,938 --> 00:07:39,848
Evolution does not work at that speed.

128
00:07:40,705 --> 00:07:43,465
So we ended up with bodies
shaped by a world where movement

129
00:07:43,545 --> 00:07:47,155
was unavoidable, living in a
world where it is optional.

130
00:07:47,948 --> 00:07:50,698
And then we act surprised
that we have to go to a gym,

131
00:07:50,808 --> 00:07:54,098
or run, or ride a bike, just
to keep the thing working.

132
00:07:57,058 --> 00:08:01,608
Which is why the sentence exercise leads
to health is slightly the wrong shape.

133
00:08:02,402 --> 00:08:06,852
When you go for a run, you may not be
adding a bonus on top of a normal body.

134
00:08:07,575 --> 00:08:11,045
You may be handing your body back
the one input it was built around.

135
00:08:11,917 --> 00:08:15,327
So perhaps the question was
never why is exercise healthy?

136
00:08:16,173 --> 00:08:18,393
Perhaps it is: why would we expect a body

137
00:08:18,473 --> 00:08:21,103
built for movement to
work properly without it?

138
00:08:21,815 --> 00:08:24,765
Movement is not something
we added to the human body.

139
00:08:25,415 --> 00:08:27,685
Movement is part of the human body.

140
00:08:30,765 --> 00:08:32,965
And exercise is good for
you might just be a long

141
00:08:33,045 --> 00:08:36,495
way of saying: this is
what normal feels like.

142
00:08:37,197 --> 00:08:38,797
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

143
00:08:38,877 --> 00:08:41,587
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

144
00:08:42,457 --> 00:08:43,867
It really helps the channel.
